Carlo:

Don't worry, this will work out.  It happens to everybody!

This will be a useful experience for you.  You who will learn about
the development process a bit.  Run install.packages in R, spotting
errors, then using yum to install additional devel packages, is a
useful experience.  For me, it is the  'usual' state of life for a
linux.

>From your errors above, it appears the first errors you hit are
related to tcl-devel and tk-devel, which any "smart" user needs
anyway.  Install them, then run the install.packages again.  Then
you'll get errors "can't find gcc" or "gfortran" and you find those.
Then you will hit some other error, probably related to 3D software
development (because Rcmdr wants "rgl").  I think the Fedora package
for that will depend on the video driver you use.  If this is a clean
computer with only a minimal desktop install, you may go through this
cycle 10 times.

If you don't know about it yet, there's a gui program called yumex
that may make it easier to find rpm packages in the repositories.

Becoming comfortable with this is an essential part of being one of us
(Linux guys) instead of one of them (members of evil empire :)))

That being said, however, I can offer one tip to reduce the work. In
recent R, the depends option has more values than true or false.  It
allows c("Depends", "Imports",   "LinkingTo", "Suggests", "Enhances")
Rcmdr suggests many packages that it does not require. changing the
install command to

install.packages("Rcmdr", dependencies=c("Depends"))

will cause R to try to build a smaller set of packages. The suggests
and enhances are spinning out of control.

You can download the Rcmdr source code from r-project.org and see for
yourself.  Note the difference:

Depends: R (>= 2.6.0), tcltk, grDevices, utils, car (>= 1.2-1),
Suggests: abind, aplpack, colorspace, effects (>= 1.0-7), foreign,
        grid, Hmisc, lattice, leaps, lmtest, MASS, mgcv, multcomp (>=
        0.991-2), nlme, nnet, relimp, rgl, RODBC


Oh, one more thing about Rcmdr.  Here's the good news if you keep
pushing through dep=T.  Once you install that,  you will have just
about everything you need to build almost any R package.  I think
that, if you get the requirements and suggestions from Rcmdr, and all
the packages it suggests or requires, and so forth, you have more than
100 of the most notable packages..


-- 
